      "embedding": null,
      "created_at": "2025-11-16T08:57:28.431367",
      "updated_at": "2025-11-16T08:57:28.431367",
      "ideal_answer": "The principles of object-oriented programming (OOP) in Java include encapsulation, inheritance, polymorphism, and abstraction. Encapsulation involves bundling data and methods that operate on that data within a single unit, or class, which enhances data security and integrity. Inheritance allows for code reusability, enabling new classes to inherit properties and methods from existing classes. Polymorphism provides the ability to process objects differently based on their data type or class, which is particularly useful in implementing interfaces and method overriding. Abstraction simplifies complex systems by exposing only the necessary parts while hiding implementation details. When developing applications, these principles influence my design decisions by promoting modularity and maintainability. For example, I often use interfaces to define contracts for services, allowing for flexible implementations. This approach not only enhances code readability but also facilitates easier testing and future enhancements. By adhering to OOP principles, I ensure that my applications are scalable and easier to manage over time.",
      "rationale": "The answer outlines the principles of OOP in Java and their influence on design decisions. Mastery is evident in the comprehensive explanation of each principle and its application in real-world scenarios, promoting modularity and maintainability. A weaker answer might provide a vague overview of OOP principles without connecting them to practical design considerations."
    },
